# Project: Event Sponsorship Marketplace (Stage 1)

A mobile-first platform where event organizers list events with sponsorship packages, sponsors browse and show interest, and both sides chat. Admin verifies organizers and approves listings. First market: Navratri and Garba events in Gujarat. Built so new cities, event types, seasons and languages are added as data, not code.

User types: organizer, sponsor, general (browse only in Stage 1), admin.

## Source of truth

Read these before building a feature. Do not invent requirements that are not in them. If something is unclear or missing, stop and ask, or note it as an open item.

- `docs/business-model.md`
- `docs/nfr.md`
- `docs/srs-stage1.md` (requirement IDs such as FR-AUTH-01, BR-03)
- `docs/database-design.md`
- `supabase/migrations/` (the database schema; it is the real data model)

## Stack

- Next.js (App Router) with TypeScript in strict mode
- Tailwind CSS and shadcn/ui
- Supabase: Postgres, Auth (phone OTP), Storage, Realtime
- next-intl for interface text in English (en), Hindi (hi) and Gujarati (gu)
- Zod for input validation
- Hosting on Vercel

Ask before adding any new dependency or service.

## Scope

- Build Stage 1 only: no deal payments, no ticket sales, no premium sponsor plan, no commission.
- The only payments are the platform's own fees (organizer listing fee, sponsor subscription), and they come last.

## Conventions

- Money is stored as integer paise. Show rupees only in the UI, with Indian number grouping.
- No hard-coded interface text. Every visible string goes in the next-intl message files for en, hi and gu.
- Mobile-first layouts. Keep pages light, because many users have average mobile networks.
- Validate every input with Zod on the server, even if the client also validates.
- Never edit a migration that has already been applied. Add a new migration instead.
- Every new table gets Row Level Security and policies in the same migration.
- Business rules live in the database or server code, not only in the UI.
- Mention the requirement ID (for example FR-EVT-05) in code comments and commit messages for non-obvious logic.
- Keep functions small, name things after the domain (organizer, sponsor, event, package, interest), and write tests for business rules and access rules.

## How to work

1. For each task, first write a short plan: files to create or change, and what you will not touch. Wait for approval.
2. Build one slice at a time, following the SRS module order. Do not start the next module on your own.
3. After the change, list what to test by hand, including a test for each user role.
4. If a requirement conflicts with the database schema, report the conflict and do not silently change either one.
